{:receipt :kotobase/h8-refuted-in-production
 :date "2026-08-18"
 :hypothesis "h8-l2-cache-api"
 :outcome :refuted
 :severity "production outage, minutes, rolled back"

 :what-was-deployed
 {:version "e36410a5-e76b-44f0-ac30-8828d24ad44c"
  :worker "kotobase-protocols-worker"
  :routes ["s3" "atproto" "pinning" "sparql" "gremlin" "graphql" ".kotobase.net"]
  :source "net-kotobase/control-plane 57b1f350f"
  :build "shadow-cljs release worker, 0 warnings; verify:waituntil OK (3 sites)"}

 :measured
 {:before {:version "120f3e08" :probe-status 501
           :wall-s-cold 3.34 :wall-s-warm 2.38}
  :after  {:version "e36410a5"
           :wall-s [18.93 32.34 41.88 31.21 14.22 40.84 30.43 27.12 40.69 24.92 16.67 24.98]
           :n 12
           :diag "sampled false on all 12 -- no isolate completed a hydrate"
           :then "probe 503 at 31.3 s"}
  :after-rollback {:version "120f3e08" :probe-status 501 :wall-s 3.51
                   :surfaces {:sparql 200 :gremlin 200 :graphql 200 :s3 200}}}

 :mechanism
 "The tier adds one `cache.match` per block before the durable read, and one
  AWAITED `cache.put` per block the durable store answered. A hydrate of this
  graph touches 84 blocks and the novelty chain forces it to touch them ONE AT A
  TIME -- the next CID does not exist until the previous block is decoded. So
  the change inserted up to 168 sequential Cache API operations into a request
  that previously made 84 R2 reads, and the request stopped fitting in a Worker."

 :the-structural-finding
 "Any PER-BLOCK auxiliary tier pays 84x on this path, and it cannot be
  amortised by batching because there is no batch -- the reads are sequential by
  construction. This is the same structural fact that already limits packing
  here (root ADR-2608160100 / iteration-04's note on h4), arriving from the
  other direction: packing cannot coalesce a chain the reader walks one link at
  a time, and neither can a cache that is consulted one link at a time."

 :the-reasoning-that-was-wrong
 {:claim "the L2 write must be awaited, because an un-awaited cache write can be
          cancelled when the request ends, and the sibling Worker awaits its own"
  :both-clauses :true
  :conclusion :false
  :why "This repo already carries `ExecutionContext.waitUntil` and a
        `verify:waituntil` gate precisely so work can outlive the response, and
        the sibling's read path is not 84 sequential misses. The right primitive
        was in the repo and was not used."
  :and-plumbing-waituntil-would-not-fix-it
  "It removes the awaits from the critical path and leaves all 84
   `cache.match` calls, so it addresses less than half of what was measured."}

 :what-the-tests-could-not-catch
 "131 tests, 828 assertions, and a break/restore that produced exactly 2
  failures in the new test and 0 collateral. All of it ran under Node with a
  FAKE `caches` whose match/put are in-memory map operations -- microseconds.
  The test proved the read path CONSULTS L2 and attributes the answer to it,
  which is what it claimed. It could not price a real Cache API call, and
  nothing in the suite had a cost floor that would notice 168 of them.

  A test that verifies a tier is reached is not a test that the tier is
  affordable, and the second question is the one the hypothesis was about."

 :what-survives
 "The idea, at a different granularity. h1-seed-materialisation is this same
  move where the path can use it: ONE object keyed by the chain CID, one read,
  one write. h8 was h1 applied per block, and per block is what this read cannot
  afford. That makes h1 more interesting than its rank suggested, not less."

 :cost-of-finding-out
 "One deploy, ~12 probe requests at 15-42 s, one 503, one rollback. The graph
  serves no organic traffic, so no user request is known to have been affected;
  that is luck about this particular graph, not a property of the process."}
